\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{Training-Free LLM Information Extraction}
\label{sec:rw_training_free}

Recent surveys categorize LLM-based IE along instruction-tuning and code-generation axes \cite{llm4ie-survey-2024}. \citet{instructuie-2023} pioneered multi-task instruction tuning for unified IE. On the generalist front, \citet{knowcoder-2024} encode structured knowledge into LLMs via code-style schemas, \citet{gollie-2024} leverage annotation guidelines for zero-shot IE, and \citet{codeie-2023} reformulate extraction as code generation. \citet{adelie-2024} align LLMs on IE through targeted instruction tuning, while \citet{oneke-2025} provide a dockerized schema-guided agent system. For structured output, \citet{slot-2025} propose schema-driven decoding that constrains LLM generation to valid extraction formats.

For NER specifically, \citet{uniner-2024} distill open NER capability from ChatGPT into smaller models, \citet{gliner-2024} introduce a bidirectional transformer for generalist NER with \citet{gliner2-2025} extending it to multi-task IE, and \citet{nuner-2024} pre-train entity recognition encoders on LLM-annotated data. Zero-shot approaches include self-improving NER through iterative refinement \cite{self-improve-ner-2024}, type-description-driven recognition \cite{zeroner-2025}, reasoning-based NER paradigms \cite{reasoning-ner-2025}, and guided synthetic data generation \cite{guidex-2025}. For relation extraction, \citet{self-prompting-re-2024} use self-prompting to enable zero-shot relation classification, and \citet{code4uie-2024} augment code-generation IE with retrieval. \citet{familiarity-2025} highlight evaluation pitfalls in zero-shot NER by quantifying label shifts between synthetic training data and target schemas.

These methods demonstrate strong performance on benchmarks with moderate schema sizes (typically 4--20 types). However, none systematically investigates how performance degrades as schema complexity scales---either in the number of candidate types or in inter-type semantic similarity. Our work addresses this gap with controlled experiments isolating the effects of schema size and type confusability on training-free LLM extraction.

\subsection{Schema Complexity in IE}
\label{sec:rw_schema}

The most directly related work is LLM-PEE \cite{llm-pee-2025}, which addresses event extraction with massive type inventories by partitioning schemas based on type embedding similarity. LLM-PEE clusters event types and assigns each cluster to a separate extraction prompt, demonstrating improved performance on schemas with hundreds of types. Our work is complementary in three respects. First, whereas LLM-PEE develops an engineering solution---partition to improve extraction---we conduct controlled empirical analysis to understand \emph{why} extraction degrades. Second, LLM-PEE does not disentangle the effects of type count from type confusability; their partitioning simultaneously reduces both. Our BCS-controlled experiments (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) isolate these as independent degradation channels. Third, our pairwise SCS analysis provides a theoretical explanation for why their similarity-based partitioning works: it reduces Batch Confusability Scores, directly targeting the Channel~2 degradation mechanism. Our SDGD experiments (\S\ref{sec:sdgd}) further reveal that the merge step---not partitioning quality---is the bottleneck in decompose-then-merge pipelines, a limitation that applies to LLM-PEE's approach as well.

Several other approaches address schema complexity from different angles. \citet{iepile-2024} construct a large-scale IE corpus using hard negative mining during training, where confusable types are deliberately included to improve robustness---related to our confusability analysis but in a supervised setting. \citet{parse-2025} rewrite and optimize schema definitions to improve extraction reliability, addressing the quality rather than the structure of type descriptions. \citet{dee-2024} decompose event schemas into sub-components before enriching and extracting, treating schema complexity through structural decomposition rather than confusability analysis. \citet{crossagentie-2025} employ multi-agent debate for cross-type IE, resolving errors \emph{post hoc} through reactive discussion rather than our proactive analysis of degradation causes. \citet{cmas-ner-2025} similarly use cooperative multi-agent frameworks for zero-shot NER. \citet{spt-2025} parameterize schemas as callable tools, abstracting complexity behind a tool-use interface, while \citet{dlisc-2025} target on-device schema-aware IE under efficiency constraints. \citet{kggen-2025} generate knowledge graphs under schema constraints, facing analogous scaling challenges when the target ontology grows.

All these methods treat schema complexity as an engineering problem to mitigate. We instead treat it as a scientific question: what are the independent mechanisms by which schema properties degrade extraction, and how can they be quantified?

\subsection{Representation Degeneration and Embedding Anisotropy}
\label{sec:rw_anisotropy}

Our centrality confound analysis (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}) connects to the representation degeneration literature. \citet{gao-repr-degen-2019} showed that word embeddings trained with language modeling objectives degenerate into a narrow cone, occupying only a subset of available directions. \citet{ethayarajh-contextual-2019} extended this to contextual representations, demonstrating that BERT, ELMo, and GPT-2 embeddings are highly anisotropic, with upper layers occupying increasingly narrow cones.

Crucially, \citet{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021} showed that while the global embedding space is anisotropic, local clusters can be approximately isotropic. This cluster-level analysis directly explains our MAVEN results: when all 168 event types derive from a single ontology (FrameNet), their embeddings form one dense cluster where a type's mean confusability conflates semantic similarity with centrality. \citet{godey-anisotropy-inherent-2024} proved that anisotropy is mathematically inherent to self-attention, not merely a training artifact, suggesting the centrality confound will persist across architectures. \citet{rajaee-cluster-isotropy-2021} proposed removing dominant directions to improve isotropy, a technique that could inform centrality correction for same-domain schemas.

Our work provides the first empirical demonstration that representation anisotropy creates a measurable confound in IE schema analysis, manifesting as a sign reversal in the confusability--error correlation for homogeneous ontologies.

\subsection{Context Length and Position Effects}
\label{sec:rw_position}

\citet{lost-in-middle-2024} demonstrated that LLMs attend preferentially to information at the beginning and end of long contexts, with retrieval accuracy degrading for items positioned in the middle. This positional bias has direct implications for IE schema prompts: when dozens of type definitions are listed sequentially, types in the middle positions may receive reduced attention. While we control for type ordering through random batching across trials, we do not fully investigate positional effects as an independent degradation channel. Disentangling positional attention decay from confusability-driven type confusion in large-schema IE prompts remains an open direction for future work.

